Build configuration values let a project or source module receive typed primitive values before its modules are compiled.

CLI numeric spelling stays raw until the exact contract selects its type. `$config` inputs use these exact numeric rules. See @../progress (Progress) for implementation status.

### `$config` declarations

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    analytics #Bool = false

    if analytics:
        send_analytics()
    ;
]

The name is the input name, the type is the contract and `=` is the fallback. A required non-optional input may omit the initializer and then reports a missing-input diagnostic when no value is supplied. An optional input with no initializer or with `= none` resolves to absence. Ordinary constants still need one.

Only `$config` declarations create explicit-only contracts. A `project` record field named `version` does not supply a source input named `version`.

### Command examples

[codeblock, $code "bash":
    moth build . --input enabled=true
    moth build . --input retries=4
    moth build . --input api_url=https://example.com
]

Raw CLI numeric spelling keeps its category until the exact input contract is known. A whole literal can materialise directly as `U64` or `F32`. Decimal/exponent spelling cannot satisfy an integer contract, and every numeric value must fit its destination's range or finite precision. Already typed programmatic inputs keep their exact type: an `I32` value cannot satisfy `I64` or `F64`. Inputs accept all eleven fixed numeric types, but not `Byte` or `Dec`. See @../numbers/#numeric-types (Numeric types) for the exact inventory.

Build configuration cannot inspect operating-system, architecture, backend or target identity.
